# WeBWorK problem written by Alex Jordan 
# Portland Community College
# ENDDESCRIPTION




##############################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserNumberWithUnits.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
);

##############################################
TEXT(beginproblem());


Context("Numeric");

$longer = random(10,20,1);
$togetherTime = random(20,30,1);
$b = $longer - 2*$togetherTime;
$c = -$longer*$togetherTime;
$oldTime = (-$b+sqrt(($b)**2-4*($c)))/2;
$oldTime = round($oldTime*100)/100;
$newTime = $oldTime+$longer;

$t = $togetherTime;
$s = $newTime;
$f = $oldTime;
$A = $longer;

$longer = NumberWithUnits("$longer","hr");
$togetherTime = NumberWithUnits("$togetherTime","hr");
$oldTime = NumberWithUnits("$oldTime","hr");
$newTime = NumberWithUnits("$newTime","hr");


##############################################

Context()->texStrings;

BEGIN_TEXT


It takes one painter \($longer\) longer to paint a house than it does a more experienced painter.  Together they can paint the house in \($togetherTime\).  How long does it take for each painter to paint the house working alone?
$PAR

The more experienced painter takes \{ans_rule(15)\} to paint the house alone, and the less experienced painter takes \{ans_rule(15)\}.



END_TEXT

Context()->normalStrings;

##############################################
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

ANS( $oldTime->cmp(tolerance=>0.01));
ANS( $newTime->cmp(tolerance=>0.01));

###########################################

$com = Formula("$t*x*(x+$A)");
$tl = $t*$A;
$t2 = 2*$t;
$c = $A-$t2;
$s2 = $tl/$f;
$cN = -$c;
$d1 = ($c)**2-4*(-$tl);
$x2 = Round(($cN-sqrt($d1))/2,2);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Let's understand how to use fractions to model work problems. Assume it takes a person [`5`] hours to complete a job. If we treat the whole job as one whole, each hour, this person would complete [`\frac{1}{5}`] of the job.

Similarly, if it takes a person [`x`] hours to complete a job, each hour, this person would complete [`\frac{1}{x}`] of the job.

Now we are ready to model this situation with math.

Assume that, if the experienced painter paints the house alone, it would take [`x`] hours to complete the job. Each hour, the experiended painter does [`\frac{1}{x}`] of the whole job.

It's given that, if the slower painter works alone, it would take [`[$A]`] more hours to complete the job. So it would take the slower painter [`x+[$A]`] hours to complete the job. Each hour, the slower painter does [`\frac{1}{x+[$A]}`] of the whole job.

It's given that, if they work together, it took them [`[$t]`] hours to complete the job. Each hour, they can complete [`\frac{1}{[$t]}`] of the whole job.

Now we can write an equation and solve it:

    [``
\begin{aligned}
  &\frac{1}{x}+\frac{1}{x+[$A]} = \frac{1}{[$t]} \\
  &\frac{1}{x} \cdot [$com] +\frac{1}{x+[$A]} \cdot [$com] = \frac{1}{[$t]} \cdot [$com] \\
  &[$t](x+[$A]) + [$t]x = x(x+[$A]) \\
  &[$t]x+[$tl] +[$t]x = x^{2}+[$A]x \\
  & 0= x^{2}+[$c]x -[$tl] \\
\end{aligned}
    ``]

In the second step, to get rid of all fractions, we multiplied each term with the common denominator [`[$com]`].

When numbers are big, we use the quadratic formula to solve the quadratic equation:

    [``
\begin{aligned}
   x &= \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\
   x &= \frac{-([$c]) \pm \sqrt{([$c])^{2}-4 \cdot (1) \cdot (-[$tl])}}{2 \cdot (1)} \\
   x &= \frac{[$cN] \pm \sqrt{[$d1]}}{2} \\
   x &= \frac{[$cN] + \sqrt{[$d1]}}{2} \:\: or \:\: x= \frac{[$cN] - \sqrt{[$d1]}}{2} \\
   x &\approx [$f] \:\: or \:\: x \approx [$x2]
\end{aligned}
    ``]

In this situation, [`x=[$x2]`] is not a valid solution, since hours cannot be negative. [`x=[$f]`] is the only solution. It would take the more experienced painter [`[$f]`] hours to complete the job.

Since, if the less experienced painter works alone, it would take [`[$A]`] more hours to complete the job. So it would take the less experienced painter [`[$f]+[$A]=[$s]`] hours to complete the job.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
